\section{Preprocessing}{

Factor predictors are converted to dummy variables
(\code{predictor_indicators = "traditional"}); no intercept column is added.
Spectral preprocessing (SNV, derivatives, smoothing, ...) is best done in a
recipe so it is re-estimated within each resample.
}

\section{Fitted object}{

\code{\link[parsnip:extract_fit_engine]{parsnip::extract_fit_engine()}} returns an ordinary \pkg{pls} \code{"mvr"}
object, so \code{\link[pls:scores]{pls::scores()}}, \code{\link[pls:loadings]{pls::loadings()}}, \code{\link[stats:coef]{stats::coef()}},
\code{\link[pls:R2]{pls::R2()}}, \code{\link[pls:RMSEP]{pls::RMSEP()}} and the \pkg{pls} plots work on it. For
classification it has extra elements \code{class_levels}, \code{prob_method} and
\code{lda} (a list of LDA fits, one per number of components). The \code{call}
element is removed (it would store the whole training set).
}

\section{Parallel processing}{

The engine is registered when \pkg{plsnip} is loaded, and \pkg{plsnip} is
declared as a dependency of the engine, so parallel workers used by
\pkg{tune} load it automatically.
